TASK 09 – IMPLEMENT AND TEST THE FARE LOGIC

1. Aim

To implement the metro smart-card fare calculation using C++ and test normal journeys, peak hours, boundary conditions, balance limits and invalid inputs.

2. Fare Rules

Zone| Base Fare
Inner| Rs 10
Middle| Rs 20
Outer| Rs 30

- Peak surcharge: 20%.
- Morning peak: 8 AM to before 10 AM.
- Evening peak: 5 PM to before 8 PM.
- Minimum remaining balance: Rs 50.

3. Data Types Used

- enum class: Represents metro zones.
- int: Stores hours and percentages.
- long long: Stores money in paise.
- bool: Checks peak hours and travel permission.
- string: Stores test descriptions.

4. Peak-Hour Expression

(hour >= 8 && hour < 10) ||
(hour >= 17 && hour < 20)

This expression correctly identifies both peak periods.

5. Test Cases

Test| Expected result
Inner off-peak| Rs 10
Inner peak| Rs 12
Middle off-peak| Rs 20
Middle peak| Rs 24
Outer off-peak| Rs 30
Outer peak| Rs 36
8:00 AM| Peak
10:00 AM| Off-peak
5:00 PM| Peak
8:00 PM| Off-peak
Exactly Rs 50 remaining| Allowed
Unknown zone| Rejected
Negative recharge| Rejected
Invalid hour input| Rejected

6. Five-Journey Calculation

Rs 10 + Rs 24 + Rs 30 + Rs 12 + Rs 36

Total = Rs 112.00

7. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

Instead of separate hand-written test cases, a table of test inputs and expected outputs can be created.

A loop processes every test case and compares the actual result with the expected result.

- Time complexity: O(t), where t is the number of tests.
- Space complexity: O(t), for storing the test table.

A new boundary test can easily be added by inserting another test case.

8. Conclusion

The metro smart-card fare system calculates fares using integer paise and applies the peak surcharge correctly. It checks the minimum balance before allowing a journey. Boundary conditions and invalid inputs are considered to improve reliability.